meta {
  name: Upload with missing file part
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/upload
  body: multipartForm
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  X-Message-Digest: 4a1f8c5071de06cc2542958aa773881595cf2785251863b77235cb819429bd14
  Content-Type: multipart/form-data
  Accept: application/json
  x-correlation-id: {{$guid}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  accountNumber: 123456789
  date: 2025-11-07
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

tests {
  test("rejects a request with no file part", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
    expect(res.getBody().errorCode).to.equal('INVALID_REQUEST');
  });
}

docs {
  Verified against the running service: this returns 400 INVALID_REQUEST ("Required part 'file' is not present"),
  not MISSING_FILE. Spring's own multipart resolver throws MissingServletRequestPartException when a required part
  is absent, which is caught by GlobalExceptionHandler's generic ErrorResponse handling before the controller body
  (and therefore ValidationUtil.validateFileNotEmpty, which throws MISSING_FILE) ever runs. MISSING_FILE is
  reachable only for a present-but-empty file part: validateFileNotEmpty now runs first in
  validateFileUploadInputs, so a zero-byte part returns 400 MISSING_FILE rather than a magic-number failure.
}
